"""Render the full-bank evaluation progress and, later, reviewed results."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
from html import escape as e
from site_navigation import project_tabs,experiment_subtabs
from build_answering_results import CSS,table,link,text
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
def main():
 d=json.loads((ROOT/'research/full-answering/progress.json').read_text());body='<header class="hero"><p class="small">Full-bank evaluation · '+e(d['updated'])+'</p><h1>S1 and S2 on all 105 questions</h1></header><section id="findings"><h2>Current findings and progress</h2><ul>'+''.join('<li>'+e(x['text'])+' '+link(x['url'],'Details')+'</li>' for x in d['brief'])+'</ul><p>'+link('../todo.html','Project checklist')+' · '+link('../daily-log.html','Daily log')+' · '+link('structured-notes.html','Completed note-optimization pilot')+'</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="coverage"><h2>1. Evaluation set and coverage</h2><p>All 105 existing questions are retained across 26 meetings and earnings calls. Each S1 model receives the same source recording and unchanged inserted question, starting with “Assistant,”. The source prefixes total 30.71 hours per model in the full-input condition.</p>'+table(1,'Execution and validation are tracked separately.',['Work','Current status','Accuracy status'],[[e(x['work']),e(x['status']),e(x['validation'])] for x in d['coverage']])+'</section>'
 body+='<section id="s1"><h2>2. Duplex-only models</h2><p>DuplexOmni, Moshi and MiniCPM process their native audio packets in order. Model outputs enter the native conversation state before the next packet. The three conditions supply only the question, the preceding four minutes plus the question, or all preceding audio plus the question. Question timing within the native packets is held fixed. The main output is the model’s native text during the 19.2 seconds following the supplied input.</p><p>All input audio is processed in the full-input condition. Retained history follows each model’s capacity: Moshi and MiniCPM use their native caches; DuplexOmni retains all turns until its context or audio-count limit and then removes the oldest complete turns to half capacity. Every removal is logged. Empty answers, incomplete inputs, context-limit events and software failures receive separate counts.</p>'+table(2,'S1 conditions; each row is run for all three models.',['Condition','Audio input','Questions per model'],[['Request only','Inserted spoken question, with native packet alignment.','105'],['Recent history','Latest 240 seconds of original discussion plus question.','105'],['Full input','All original audio through the insertion time plus question.','105']])+'<p>Before scaling DuplexOmni, the JSON and binary endpoints produced identical native fields and saved history for all 104 packet records in two short controls. Waveform synthesis can therefore be omitted for this text evaluation. Stopping decoding immediately after ASR changed later responses and was excluded. The calibration completed all six cases; its final display failed because a runtime field was missing.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="s2"><h2>3. Text S2 conditions</h2><p>The readers are Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 and Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507. A common Instruct writer generates the saved summaries and notes for both readers before seeing any future question. The reader receives the complete available transcript, the applicable saved notes and the question. Thinking uses seeds 23 and 47; Instruct uses greedy decoding.</p>'+table(3,'Nine ground-truth-transcript conditions; each uses all 105 questions.',['Condition','Saved material at question time','Purpose'],[['Reactive','None.','Baseline without earlier reasoning.'],['Reactive + focused prompt','None.','Measure the effect of the shorter, evidence-focused reader instruction.'],['Summaries · all snapshots','Every valid earlier summary that fits.','Reproduce accumulated-summary prompting.'],['Summary · latest snapshot','Only the latest valid summary.','Remove duplicated older summaries without changing writer work.'],['Structured notes · all snapshots','Every valid earlier structured snapshot that fits.','Reproduce the original structured-note method.'],['Structured notes · latest snapshot','Only the latest valid structured snapshot.','Test whether older snapshots help.'],['Source notes','One bounded state of selected original speech and relationships.','Test incremental, deduplicated source selection.'],['Source notes + focused prompt','The same source state.','Test focused reading with saved evidence.'],['Source notes without links + focused prompt','Identical selected source speech; relationship links removed.','Isolate the contribution of links between statements.']])+'<p>Source-note updates use new transcript segments, ten seconds of earlier reference speech and one current state, with at most 64 selected segments, 20 visible links and 4,096 compact-JSON tokens. The existing 25–40 second update schedule is within the requested 10–40 second range. Summaries and old structured notes use the original full-prefix and earlier-snapshot prompts. Earlier generation costs are shared when conditions reuse the same notes.</p><p>Both readers have a common 49,152-token input limit within a 65,536-token context. If accumulated snapshots exceed that input limit, the oldest snapshots are omitted and their IDs are recorded; the transcript is retained. No reference answer enters the writer or reader input.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="latency"><h2>4. Post-request latency and token cost</h2><p>The primary timing comparison uses isolated, serial Thinking requests for all 105 ground-truth-transcript questions, with seed 23. It measures time to the first answer text and time to the completed output. The prefix cache is cleared before each request and condition order is shuffled. Batched accuracy-run times are retained as throughput measurements.</p><p>Native thinking tokens count reasoning generated after the request; amortized-thinking tokens count earlier note generation and revision checks. Answer output and all earlier input/output costs are reported separately. Instruct does not expose a separate thinking channel. A reduction in token count will be called a latency improvement only when the measured time also falls and answer quality is retained.</p><p>The planned figures show paired per-question changes, accuracy against post-request thinking and wall time, and total earlier versus request-time generated tokens. Meeting-level confidence intervals will keep repeated questions and seeds from being treated as independent meetings.</p></section>'
 qd=json.loads((ROOT/'research/full-answering/timing-qualification.json').read_text());qids=list(dict.fromkeys(x['question_id'] for x in qd['cases']));qr=[]
 for qid in qids:
  pair={x['condition']:x for x in qd['cases'] if x['question_id']==qid};a=pair['reactive_guided'];b=pair['linked_guided'];qr.append([e(a['question']),str(a['native_thinking_tokens']),f"{a['latency_seconds']['first_answer_text']:.2f}",str(b['native_thinking_tokens']),f"{b['latency_seconds']['first_answer_text']:.2f}"])
 body+='<section id="timing-check"><h3>Timing qualification: two real questions</h3><p>Four actual answers qualify the streaming measurements. Source checking confirms the necessary facts in these answers. Source notes reduce response time for one question and increase it for the other; the full 105-question comparison is pending.</p>'+table(4,'Qualification examples; latency is seconds from request submission to first answer text.',['Question','Reactive thinking tokens','Reactive latency (s)','Source-note thinking tokens','Source-note latency (s)'],qr)
 for x in qd['cases']:
  body+='<details><summary>'+e(x['question'])+' · '+('Source notes' if x['condition']=='linked_guided' else 'Reactive')+'</summary>'+text(x['answer'])+'<p><strong>Original speech:</strong></p><ul>'+''.join('<li>'+e(s['speaker']+': '+s['text'])+'</li>' for s in x['evidence'])+'</ul></details>'
 body+='<p>'+link('../research/full-answering/timing-qualification.json','Exact final answers and recorded timestamps')+'</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="asr"><h2>5. Generated transcripts and speaker identities</h2><p>The full extension includes Parakeet and Whisper, causal audio prefixes and stable speaker labels. Automatic requester matching is compared with a separately labeled diagnostic that uses past annotations to map speaker identities. Notes retain stable anonymous labels; the diagnostic supplies a consistent identity legend at question time.</p><p>The four primary ASR comparisons use the same original reader prompt: reactive, all summaries, all structured-note snapshots and source notes. Word times assign recognized words to speakers and prevent future speech from entering earlier updates.</p><p>The variable-speaker pipeline completed six earnings-call prefix tests without receiving the reference speaker count. It estimated six speakers in the first call and five in the second; the reference annotations contain six and nine labels respectively. Full-bank ASR, WER and speaker-identity checks remain pending.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="evaluation"><h2>6. Reference and scoring checks</h2><p>A new Thinking-model audit read the complete available transcript for all 105 questions. Codex checked the nine flagged chronology, identity and scope claims against the original speech and froze the references for scorer qualification. Agreement with the audit model does not by itself validate a reference. <a href="../research/full-answering/reference-flag-review.json">Source checks for all nine flags</a>.</p><p>The proposed main score checks the minimum facts needed to answer the question and reports material unsupported claims separately. Missing optional qualifications are recorded separately from wrong answers. A supported-correct score requires the necessary facts and no material unsupported claim. Previous strict scores remain available for comparison. The scorer must pass source-reviewed real examples and controlled answer changes before full-bank accuracy is reported.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="resources"><h2>7. Compute and next steps</h2><p>Each submitted job requests one GPU. Moshi, MiniCPM, the text writer and text readers each use one H100 80 GB with their components on GPU 0. DuplexOmni qualification used one A100 80 GB with the complete S1 stack on GPU 0; the full native-text run uses one H100 80 GB for the Thinker. Dependent jobs reuse a lane, with at most four GPUs active. vLLM supplies BF16 inference, CUDA graphs and prefix caching for text models.</p><ul>'+''.join('<li>'+e(x)+'</li>' for x in d['next_steps'])+'</ul></section>'
 page='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark"><meta name="description" content="Full-bank S1 and S2 comparison: answer accuracy, post-request latency and thinking-token cost."><title>Full-bank S1 and S2 evaluation · AI2AI Duplex</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css"><style>'+CSS+'</style></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a class="brand" href="../index.html">AI2AI Duplex</a>'+project_tabs('experiments','../')+'</div></header><div class="container collection-nav">'+experiment_subtabs('full-answering')+'</div><main id="main" class="container results">'+body+'</main></body></html>\n';(ROOT/'experiments/full-answering-results.html').write_text(page)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
